one it's it's not expected that  someone's going to be able to learn all  the course content and do all the  assignments like purely on their own I  don't think that's a smart thing to do  at all so the way I see it is that  actually if you're studying at  University then the most important skill  that's going to help you get good grades  is being able to socialise and being  able to make friends with strangers and  and communicate and work in a team now I  know that for like a lot of you guys  study maths or physics that subject like  that because that's what I talk about on  my channel and often socializing isn't  our most favorite activity in fact like  I in person I'm an incredibly shy and  socially awkward person so if someone  gave me this advice ages ago it would  probably horrify me and I would say no  like learning physics shouldn't be about  how good you are at socializing it  should be all about your inherent like  mathematical skills and you should be  able to be like that loan genius and and  you would think like Einstein never had  to work in a team he just came up with  all these series on his own but you know  like I've been through the Uni system  and I've sort of seen how this works and  I've come to the realization that there  is no such thing as the lone genius but  that's sort of a whole nother topic  essentially the way that a lot of Muniz  work in the way at least mine definitely  did was your to get the most out of the  course and to get the most out of what  the lecturer is offering you and and all  the opportunities available to you you  really need to work with other people  and if you don't have friends in a class  it's going to be really hard for you no  matter how smart you think you are so my  biggest study tip is to find some  friends early in your course and you  know if you don't know how to find  friends here's some ideas like the most  valuable friends I guess I made at  university were the people that I met in  like the front row of lectures it seems  to be then the people that sit in the  front row are like really keen and often  really friendly really open to like I  don't know working together and and  having chats about the content and I  think in the front row of lectures you  can find some really valuable people I  think uni and finding friends is always  harder for people that don't live on  care  and don't have that sort of automatic  friend group that comes along with like  living with other people but I think it  really is possible to meet friends and  Lich's it can be as easy as just like  asking them what their major is and then  asking them you know about some  assignment you have guys have coming up  whether they like to work together and  the more momentum you can build around  sort of a study group and like add more  people into it and and really build that  family and that sense of belonging and  and comradeship like around your course  it's gonna be a lot better for you so  let me give you some examples from my  life and what I'm talking about here now  today is actually my 23rd birthday and  it's the anniversary of a time back in  first year when I spent my birthday like  having a real miserable time in a  mathematical modelling lad it was an 8  a.m. lab on a Friday and I mean that  timing is awful about why this was a  really awful day for me it was because I  just had no friends in this class I  guess the content wasn't even that  difficult like I remember this  particular lab we were on MATLAB and we  were like trying to code in some  functions to like generate trig  functions like the sine function through  a Taylor expansion we were coding it and  like I feel like this would kind of be  really easy for me to do now it's not  even hard in concept but at the time I  just had like no idea what I was doing I  would I don't know how to use MATLAB I  didn't know anything about Taylor  expansion but I also just had no friends  in this class and it was the only class  like this because it was the only class  I was taking different to like my main  group of like solid friends who had made  in my other courses and I was just like  sitting in this room and everyone felt  really unapproachable I didn't know  anyone and I just feel like I had to  struggle through it alone and I was like  that's fine I can just like read up you  know explanations for this and try and  figure it out myself but honestly it was  just miserable like I took so long to  try and figure it out and if I just had  like someone beside me who I felt  comfortable like talking to and being  like oh like I you up to the Stepp like  how do you think we go from here it  would just would have been so much  better  and it was actually after I had such a  bad time in that course but I was like  I'm never gonna let that happen again  I'm always going to make sure you know  in the first week of a new course my  priority is going to be making sure I  have a friend in this class like that I  know someone and that I can I can always  ask someone for help and I think that  was really so important for me was  prioritizing not letting myself be alone  and struggling alone in a course the way  that I would work with my friends who I  made in classes that maybe we'd like sit  together in lectures and maybe joke  around a little bit or like talk about  around them stuff but then like when it  came to doing assignments often there  would be like facebook messenger chats  or groups or something and that would be  like you know how do you do this certain  type of question and then people would  sort of explain it to you and and it  really worked both ways because if you  were struggling with something you could  ask your friends for help and they would  often you know get you out of the rut  you're in much faster than you could get  out yourself but also if you are the  person who already understands what's  going on and you'd probably could just  do this particular assignment all by  yourself no worries I think it's really  valuable for you to help other people  out and to not be a jerk but to you know  really bring people along with you and I  think it really benefits you because you  are getting practice explaining  something and you're actually making  sure you understand it better yourself  you're solidifying things and you've got  these people who sort of like are really  grateful for your help so I really think  it goes both ways if you're struggling  in class make it your priority to make  good friends and people that you know  are aligned with what you're trying to  achieve in the class and aren't going to  drag you down and are really going to  help you get there and even if you are  doing really well maybe go out of your  way a little bit to help others to still  appreciate the value of friends when  you're studying and to know that it also  values you to explain things to other  people thanks for watching

